ORIGINAL RESEARCH

Evaluation of Effectiveness of an Educational Intervention
Program on the Knowledge, Attitude, and Practice Regarding
Use and Storage of Toothbrush among Parents and Children
Patilla Amreen Shaban1, K Reshma Pai2, Mohamed Labeeb KP3 , Mohammad Hashir4
Received on: 30 April 2025; Accepted on: 20 September 2025; Published on: 16 December 2025

A b s t r ac t
Background: Toothbrushes are crucial for oral hygiene, but improper storage and maintenance can lead to contamination, posing risks of
dental and systemic diseases. Educating children and parents on proper toothbrush care is essential to prevent contamination and promote
good oral health.
Objectives: This study aimed to assess knowledge on toothbrush use and storage among parents and children, educate on proper management,
evaluate the microbial load and storage site relationship, and assess the outcome of an educational program on toothbrush practices.
Materials and methods: A randomized controlled trial was conducted among 60 children (7–9 years) and their parents in three phases: baseline
knowledge assessment, microbial load analysis of used toothbrushes, and evaluation of an educational program’s effectiveness through a
follow-up survey.
Results: The study found a significant reduction in microbial load on toothbrushes and improvement in knowledge, attitude, and practice
regarding proper toothbrush use and storage among children and parents after the educational intervention.
Conclusion: Educational programs can significantly improve knowledge and attitudes toward proper toothbrush use and storage. Dentists
play a crucial role in promoting good oral hygiene practices, and regular toothbrush disinfection is essential for preventing infections and
maintaining overall health.
Clinical significance: This study highlights the importance of proper toothbrush storage and maintenance in preventing contamination and
promoting oral and systemic health, particularly through targeted education for parents and children.
Keywords: Educational intervention, Infection prevention, Oral hygiene, Toothbrush contamination.
International Journal of Clinical Pediatric Dentistry (2025): 10.5005/jp-journals-10005-3342

Introduction

1

Toothbrush is an essential part of everyone’s day-to-day life, but it
plays an important role in disease transmission and increases the risk of
infection.1 Most children and parents use toothbrush for cleaning teeth,
but there is very little public awareness that toothbrushes can become
contaminated with use.2 If not stored and maintained properly,
toothbrush may induce not only dental diseases but also many
systemic problems such as respiratory, gastrointestinal, cardiovascular,
septicemia, and renal problems.3 American Dental Association issued
health advisory of changing toothbrush every 3 months in 1996.4
Studies have shown that the effectiveness in removing dental plaque
reduces owing to flaring of bristles.5 However, microbial contamination
was not taken into account while advocating the number of times a
toothbrush needs to be changed.5 By maintaining good oral hygiene
and using clean disinfected toothbrushes, oral and systemic diseases
can be controlled vastly.6 After brushing, toothbrushes are mostly
rinsed with normal water and often kept in bathrooms or bathrooms
with attached toilet, which become perfect hubs for millions of
microorganisms, as they may be exposed to pathogenic bacteria that
are dispersed during flushing.7 Millions of bacteria are released in the
atmosphere through flushing of toilets.8 Toothbrushes stored together
in one container get contaminated when they rub against each
other.9 Moreover, toothbrushes should not be exchanged between

individuals, which could happen by mistake if kept in one container.6
With children, the possibility of toothbrush contamination is higher
compared to adults, and as they have less immunity than adults,

