equivalent molecular complexes. The binding strengths can be
tuned based on the choice of M and L, the oxidation state22 of
the metal center, and the stoichiometry (M:L),23 while ligand
exchange reactions in the coordination sphere of the central
metal can elicit dynamic and self-healing properties.24
Importantly, these interactions are stronger than other
noncovalent interactions and can approach the strength of
covalent bonds when optimal M−L pairs are used.25 Moreover,
while M−L interactions can be the driving force for crosslinking, they can also be supported by secondary noncovalent
interactions to further influence and tune mechanical properties.26,27 Examples of M−L interactions exploited for the
fabrication of polymeric hydrogels include (but not limited
to); Mn+�COO−,28 Mn+�imidazole,29 Mn+�(bi/ter-)pyridine,30 Mn+�catechol/pyrogallol.31,32
