The distinct combination of platinum (Pt) and arsenic (As)
containing materials exhibits significant interest, primarily
owing their historical and ongoing use in medicine, from the
use of As for thousands of years in Chinese medicine33 to the
more recent development of As- and Pt-based chemotherapeutics.34,35 In the mid-20th century, the discovery that
Pt inhibited cancer cell growth led to the development of PtII
drugs, with cisplatin36 being approved by the FDA in 1978.
Similarly, a renaissance in the use of (in)organic As
compounds was stimulated by the discovery that arsenic
trioxide (AsIII) induced remission in patients with acute
promyelocytic leukemia (APL).37 To date, PtII complexes
cisplatin, carboplatin, oxaliplatin, and arsenic trioxide are the
only nonradiative metal/metalloid complexes approved to treat
cancer by the FDA.38−40 The antimicrobial activity of As41 and
Pt36 complexes predates their clinical use as antitumor agents.
More recently, aromatic organic arsenicals have been
developed as antimicrobial additives for poultry litter,42 while
cisplatin has been shown to have an extensive antimicrobial
spectrum.43
Furthermore, Pt-complexes and (in)organic arsenicals have
exhibited diverse chemical reactivity that can be exploited in
the context of polymeric materials,44,45 including protein/
peptide−polymer bioconjugation46 as well as the fabrication of
responsive polymeric arsenical nanoparticles47−51 and hydrogels,52 nontoxic and able to support cell culture. Likewise, PtIIsalts have been used as precursors for the synthesis of Pt0
nanoparticles (PtNPs), which have potential applications in
catalysis, electronics, energy storage, and biomedical applications.53
